<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Nop.Web.Framework - fourth stage of the migration, first of the three presentation
    projects, downstream of Nop.Core, Nop.Data and Nop.Services
    (Requirements 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 2.4, 4.1, 4.2).

    ===================================================================================
    WHY Microsoft.NET.Sdk AND NOT Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor
    ===================================================================================
    The Razor SDK exists to compile .cshtml files into a view assembly (and, for a Razor
    Class Library, to package them as embedded application parts). This project contains
    NO .cshtml files at all - verified: `find . -name "*.cshtml"` returns nothing; the
    only non-.cs files in the tree were app.config and packages.config, both deleted by
    this task. All 104 files are C#.

    What this project *does* contain is C# code that BINDS AGAINST Razor/MVC types:
      * ViewEngines\Razor\WebViewPage.cs      - System.Web.Mvc.WebViewPage<TModel>
                                               subclass -> RazorPage<TModel> (task 6.3)
      * Themes\ThemeableRazorViewEngine.cs    - System.Web.Mvc.RazorViewEngine subclass
      * Themes\ThemeableVirtualPathProviderViewEngine.cs
      * HtmlExtensions.cs, UI\*, Security\Captcha\* - HtmlHelper/UrlHelper extension
                                               methods and HelperResult templates
    Every replacement type for those - Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Razor.RazorPage<T>,
    IHtmlHelper, IUrlHelper, HelperResult, TagBuilder, IHtmlContent - lives in
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Razor.dll / Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.ViewFeatures.dll /
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Razor.Runtime.dll, all of which ship inside the
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App SHARED FRAMEWORK (verified present in
    packs/Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Ref/*/ref/net10.0/ in the sdk:10.0 image).

    Referencing types from Razor therefore needs only a FrameworkReference. Compiling
    Razor files needs the Razor SDK - and there are none here.

    CONSEQUENCE FOR LATER TASKS:
      * Task 6.3 ports the Razor *infrastructure* (base page class, HTML helpers,
        view engine, tag helpers) purely as C#; no SDK change to this file is required.
      * Tasks 7.x / 8.x DO own .cshtml files (Nop.Web ~ views + Nop.Admin ~325 views).
        Those projects use Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web, which imports the Razor SDK
        automatically, so they need no explicit Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor either.
      * If a future task moves shared .cshtml (e.g. shared editor/display templates or
        a themeable layout) INTO this project, this Sdk attribute must change to
        Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor at that point.

    ===================================================================================
    DROPPED IN THE CLASSIC -> SDK CONVERSION
    ===================================================================================
      * ToolsVersion / DefaultTargets / xmlns MSBuild 2003 attributes
      * ProjectGuid {75FD4163-333C-4DD5-854D-2EF294E45D94}, ProductVersion,
        SchemaVersion, FileAlignment, AppDesignerFolder, OutputType (Library is the SDK
        default), TargetFrameworkProfile, NuGetPackageImportStamp
      * TargetFrameworkVersion v4.5.1
      * The Debug|AnyCPU / Release|AnyCPU PropertyGroup pair (the SDK supplies
        equivalent Debug/Release defaults)
      * Import of $(MSBuildToolsPath)\Microsoft.CSharp.targets, the commented
        BeforeBuild/AfterBuild stubs, the Microsoft.Bcl.Build.targets import and its
        EnsureNuGetPackageBuildImports guard target
      * The explicit <Compile Include="..."/> list of 104 files (two ItemGroups -
        the second held only Kendoui\ModelStateExtensions.cs) - replaced by the SDK's
        implicit globbing. Note the file literally named "Validators\ValidatorExtensions .cs"
        (with a space before the extension) is picked up by the glob unchanged.
      * BCL <Reference Include="System.*"/> entries: System, System.Core, System.Data,
        System.Data.DataSetExtensions, System.Xml, System.Xml.Linq, Microsoft.CSharp,
        System.Net, System.Net.Http, System.Net.Http.WebRequest,
        System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations, System.Configuration, System.ServiceModel,
        System.Web - subsumed by the net10.0 shared framework, replaced by a package
        below (System.ServiceModel -> System.ServiceModel.Syndication), or with no
        net10.0 counterpart at all (System.Web).
        System.Configuration was referenced but is unused: no file in this project names
        ConfigurationManager, so there is no appSettings surface to carry to
        IConfiguration in task 6.5.
      * <Reference/> items with HintPaths into ..\..\packages\ - the packages.config
        surface, re-declared below as version-less <PackageReference/> items where a
        net10.0 successor exists.
      * <None Include="app.config"/> and <None Include="packages.config"/>. BOTH FILES
        ARE DELETED by this task (Requirements 1.4, 1.5). app.config contained nothing
        but a <runtime>/<assemblyBinding> block of .NET Framework binding redirects for
        System.Web.Mvc, WebGrease, Antlr3.Runtime, Newtonsoft.Json, Autofac and the
        WindowsAzure/OData/Redis strong-name assemblies - i.e. an artifact of
        packages.config itself. Binding redirects do not exist on .NET; nothing in the
        file survives, and leaving it would risk the SDK emitting it as
        Nop.Web.Framework.dll.config. Task 6.5 retains ownership of the remaining
        configuration concerns (Properties\AssemblyInfo.cs and any View web.config
        fragments) - this project has no Views/ folder and therefore no View
        web.config to remove.

    TASK 6.5 AUDIT RESULT (Requirements 1.4, 1.5, 5.3) - all four items verified:
      1. Legacy config files: NONE remain anywhere under this project (recursive find for
         *.config / *.asax / *.ashx / *.axd / Global.asax*, excluding bin+obj: 0 hits).
         app.config and packages.config were the only two and 6.1 deleted both. There is
         no Views/ folder, no Areas/ folder and no App_Start/ folder, so there is no View
         web.config and no Web.Debug/Web.Release transform to remove.
      2. Config-reading API surface: NONE. No file names ConfigurationManager,
         WebConfigurationManager or System.Configuration - confirming 6.1's finding. The
         only appearances of those words in the project are inside comments (this file,
         and a doc comment in Infrastructure\NopApplicationBuilderExtensions.cs).
      3. Properties\AssemblyInfo.cs is KEPT. src/Directory.Build.props sets
         GenerateAssemblyInfo=false (task 2.3) precisely so the hand-kept files stay
         authoritative, matching Nop.Core, Nop.Data and Nop.Services. Every attribute in
         it (AssemblyTitle/Description/Configuration/Company/Product/Copyright/Trademark/
         Culture, ComVisible, Guid, AssemblyVersion, AssemblyFileVersion) is valid on
         net10.0 and none is duplicated by the SDK while GenerateAssemblyInfo is false.
         It carries NO System.Web hosting hook - no [assembly: PreApplicationStartMethod]
         and no [assembly: WebActivator...] - so nothing had to be removed (unlike
         Nop.Core's PluginManager, task 2.4 / runtime deferral 1.1).
      4. Orphans from 6.4: none. GenericPathRoute.cs, ThemeableRazorViewEngine.cs,
         ThemeableVirtualPathProviderViewEngine.cs and AsIsBundleOrderer.cs were deleted
         by 6.3/6.4 with every reference to them removed; the project compiles at 0
         errors with no unreferenced residue.
    The only genuine settings surface this project owns is the <forms> element that task
    6.4 transcribed into constants; 6.5 moved it to typed options bound from
    IConfiguration - see Infrastructure\NopAuthenticationConfig.cs.

    Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs is retained (matching Nop.Core, Nop.Data and
    Nop.Services); GenerateAssemblyInfo=false in src/Directory.Build.props keeps the SDK
    from emitting a duplicate attribute set.

    TargetFramework, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, GenerateAssemblyInfo and the shared
    assembly metadata come from src/Directory.Build.props; net10.0 is restated here so
    the target framework is verifiable from the project file itself (Requirement 1.2).
    It is deliberately the OS-agnostic net10.0 and NOT net10.0-windows. The only
    Windows-flavoured surface left in the project is Security\FilePermissionHelper.cs
    (WindowsIdentity / System.Security.AccessControl); both
    System.Security.AccessControl.dll and System.IO.FileSystem.AccessControl.dll are
    part of the cross-platform net10.0 targeting pack (verified in the sdk:10.0 image),
    so they need no package and no OS-specific TFM - they merely throw at runtime off
    Windows, exactly as the rest of the migration already accepts for Windows-first
    testing (design section 7).
    Task 6.5 made that intent explicit rather than implicit: FilePermissionHelper's
    CheckPermissions method now carries [SupportedOSPlatform("windows")], the documented
    way to declare a per-member platform constraint. This is purely declarative - no
    behaviour changed - and it takes this project from 59 warnings to 10 by clearing all
    49 CA1416 platform-compatibility warnings. It was NOT done with a NoWarn suppression,
    and the attribute is on the method rather than the class so that GetDirectoriesWrite /
    GetFilesWrite, which are platform-neutral path arithmetic, keep no constraint.
    See that file's remarks for the (intended) knock-on for tasks 7.3 / 8.3.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ASP.NET Core shared framework - the single largest replacement in this task.

    This project is saturated with System.Web: 44 files "using System.Web.Mvc", 16
    "using System.Web", 12 "using System.Web.Routing", 3 "using System.Web.WebPages",
    2 "using System.Web.Mvc.Html", 2 "using System.Web.UI". Their successors -
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc(.Core/.ViewFeatures/.Razor/.TagHelpers),
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http(.Abstractions), Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing,
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Antiforgery, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Session,
    Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory, Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration/Options -
    all ship in Microsoft.AspNetCore.App. A FrameworkReference is therefore the right
    vehicle (same decision as Nop.Core and Nop.Services); adding individual
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.* packages would duplicate shared-framework assemblies.

    In particular this covers, with no extra package, everything tasks 6.2-6.5 need:
      services.AddHttpContextAccessor()   Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http
      services.AddMemoryCache()           Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory (verified
                                          present in the AspNetCore ref pack)
      services.AddSession()               Microsoft.AspNetCore.Session
      cookie authentication + CookieSecurePolicy
                                          Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Cookies
      IHostApplicationLifetime            Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions
      IEndpointRouteBuilder / MapControllerRoute
                                          Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    packages.config -> PackageReference (Requirements 1.3, 1.5).

    Versions are governed centrally by src/Directory.Packages.props
    (ManagePackageVersionsCentrally=true, CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled=false),
    so every item below is intentionally version-less.

    Only DIRECT dependencies are declared - PackageReference is transitive by default,
    unlike packages.config which enumerated the whole closure. Each entry was verified
    against actual source usage:

      Autofac                     DependencyRegistrar.cs ("using Autofac",
                                  "using Autofac.Builder", "using Autofac.Core":
                                  ContainerBuilder, IRegistrationBuilder,
                                  IComponentRegistration) and
                                  Mvc\DependencyRegistrarExtensions.cs
      Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection
                                  NOT referenced by any source file TODAY - declared
                                  for task 6.4, which owns the DI integration in this
                                  project. See the note below.
      FluentValidation            NopValidatorFactory.cs (AttributedValidatorFactory,
                                  IValidator, FluentValidation.Attributes.ValidatorAttribute),
                                  Validators\BaseNopValidator.cs (AbstractValidator<T>),
                                  Validators\CreditCardPropertyValidator.cs and
                                  Validators\DecimalPropertyValidator.cs
                                  (PropertyValidator, PropertyValidatorContext),
                                  "Validators\ValidatorExtensions .cs"
                                  (IRuleBuilder/IRuleBuilderOptions, SetValidator)
      Newtonsoft.Json             Mvc\ConverterJsonResult.cs and Mvc\NullJsonResult.cs
                                  (JsonSerializerSettings / JsonConvert) and
                                  Security\Captcha\GReCaptchaValidator.cs
                                  ("using Newtonsoft.Json.Linq", JObject)
      System.Linq.Dynamic.Core    Kendoui\QueryableExtensions.cs and
                                  Validators\BaseNopValidator.cs - both currently
                                  "using System.Linq.Dynamic" (DynamicExpression.ParseLambda,
                                  IQueryable.Where/OrderBy string overloads). The
                                  namespace/API change to System.Linq.Dynamic.Core
                                  (DynamicExpressionParser.ParseLambda) is task 6.2/6.3
                                  work, exactly as Nop.Services did for Tokenizer.cs.
      System.ServiceModel.Syndication
                                  RssActionResult.cs ("using System.ServiceModel.Syndication":
                                  SyndicationFeed, GetRss20Formatter,
                                  AttributeExtensions/ElementExtensions). The net10.0
                                  targeting pack ships System.ServiceModel.Web.dll but
                                  NOT System.ServiceModel.Syndication.dll (verified), so
                                  a package is genuinely required here.

    Note on Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection: task 6.1's own description names it
    as the replacement for Autofac.Mvc5, and task 6.4 - "Integrate Autofac via
    Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection ... remove Autofac.Integration.Mvc
    per-request plumbing" - is scoped to THIS project. It is also the package that makes
    runtime-deferrals.md item 1.3 fixable here: assigning
      ContainerManager.CurrentScopeProvider =
          () => httpContextAccessor.HttpContext?.RequestServices.GetService<ILifetimeScope>()
    only works because Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection's AutofacServiceProvider
    is what backs RequestServices and exposes ILifetimeScope. It is declared here rather
    than deferred so 6.4 does not have to reopen the project file. If 6.4 concludes the
    integration belongs solely in Nop.Web's Program.cs (task 7.2), this entry should be
    removed to keep the direct-dependency rule honest.

    Dropped from the legacy packages.config / <Reference> list and NOT restated:

      Microsoft.AspNet.Mvc 5.2.3          System.Web.Mvc / .Razor / .WebPages /
      Microsoft.AspNet.Razor 3.2.3        .WebPages.Razor / .WebPages.Deployment /
      Microsoft.AspNet.WebPages 3.2.3     System.Web.Helpers have no net10.0 build and
                                          no successor package: they ARE the System.Web
                                          MVC 5 stack. Superseded wholesale by the
                                          Microsoft.AspNetCore.App FrameworkReference
                                          above (design section 5).
      Autofac.Mvc5 4.0.1                  Autofac.Integration.Mvc - AutofacDependencyResolver,
                                          RequestLifetimeScopeProvider,
                                          ContainerBuilder.RegisterControllers - is
                                          MVC5-only and has no net10.0 release.
                                          DependencyRegistrar.cs is its only consumer.
                                          Replaced by Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection
                                          plus ASP.NET Core's own request scope
                                          (task 6.4; design section 5).
      EntityFramework 6.1.3               Referenced only so DependencyRegistrar.cs
                                          could construct NopObjectContext; no file in
                                          this project names System.Data.Entity. EF Core
                                          now arrives transitively through the Nop.Data
                                          ProjectReference, so nothing is declared here.
      Microsoft.AspNet.Web.Optimization   *** NO net10.0 SUCCESSOR - SEE THE GAP NOTE
      WebGrease 1.6.0                     *** BELOW. Deliberately left out; the
      Antlr 3.5.0.2                       *** resulting compile errors in UI\PageHeadBuilder.cs
                                          *** and UI\AsIsBundleOrderer.cs are left
                                          *** standing for task 6.3 to decide on.
      WebActivator 1.5.2                  A System.Web PreApplicationStartMethod
                                          bootstrapper with no ASP.NET Core equivalent
                                          (the same hosting hook already removed from
                                          Nop.Core's PluginManager - deferral 1.1). No
                                          file in this project references it at all.
      Microsoft.Web.Infrastructure        Transitive-only (DynamicModuleUtility for
                                          System.Web); no net10.0 equivalent, no usage.
      Microsoft.Bcl 1.1.10                net45-era BCL polyfills (Task/async,
      Microsoft.Bcl.Async 1.0.168         HttpClient) plus the MSBuild targets package
      Microsoft.Bcl.Build 1.0.21          that enforced them - all subsumed by the
      Microsoft.Net.Http 2.2.29           net10.0 shared framework. Security\Captcha\
                                          GReCaptchaValidator.cs keeps "using
                                          System.Net.Http" and binds to the in-box
                                          HttpClient.
      System.Linq.Dynamic 1.0.7           net40-only and unmaintained; replaced by
                                          System.Linq.Dynamic.Core above.

    ===================================================================================
    *** KNOWN GAP - NO VIABLE net10.0 PATH: System.Web.Optimization (bundling) ***
    ===================================================================================
    Microsoft.AspNet.Web.Optimization 1.1.3 (System.Web.Optimization) with its WebGrease
    + Antlr3.Runtime minification back end is a System.Web feature. There is no .NET
    Core / .NET 10 release of any of the three, and Microsoft published no successor.
    Two files bind to it directly:
      * UI\AsIsBundleOrderer.cs  - implements IBundleOrderer; BundleContext, BundleFile
      * UI\PageHeadBuilder.cs    - BundleTable.Bundles / BundleTable.EnableOptimizations,
                                   ScriptBundle, StyleBundle, Bundle.Orderer,
                                   Bundle.Include, Styles.Render/Scripts.Render paths
    NO substitute has been invented and NO package was guessed. The two files will not
    compile; that is intentional and left for task 6.3 to resolve with an explicit
    decision. Options, for the record:
      (a) DROP BUNDLING. Emit individual <script>/<link> tags from IPageHeadBuilder and
          make SeoSettings.EnableJsBundling / EnableCssBundling inert. Smallest change;
          IPageHeadBuilder's public shape (the excludeFromBundle parameters) can stay so
          the ~hundreds of view call sites in Nop.Web/Nop.Admin are untouched. This is
          close to what upstream nopCommerce 4.0 did on its own ASP.NET Core port.
      (b) LigerShark.WebOptimizer.Core (MIT, maintained, net10.0). Real bundling and
          minification via IAssetPipeline + app.UseWebOptimizer(); PageHeadBuilder's
          bundle-registration logic is rewritten against a different model, and the
          pipeline must be configured in Nop.Web's Program.cs (task 7.2).
      (c) Hand-rolled bundler over NUglify (MIT, maintained, net10.0 - the maintained
          successor to Microsoft's Ajax Minifier, which is what WebGrease wrapped).
          Keeps PageHeadBuilder's existing "compute a virtual path, cache the combined
          file" structure; most code to write.
    Recommendation for 6.3: (a) to reach the 6.6 gate, with (b) scheduled as a
    follow-up, because bundling is a performance concern and not a correctness one.

    Packages the task brief asked to watch for and which are NOT present in this
    project's packages.config - confirmed absent, nothing to decide:
    MiniProfiler, DotNetOpenAuth, Microsoft.AspNet.Mvc.Futures (Mvc2Futures),
    Microsoft.AspNet.WebApi*, AutoMapper (no file here references it).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Autofac" />
    <PackageReference Include="Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Linq.Dynamic.Core" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.ServiceModel.Syndication" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ProjectReferences to the already-migrated SDK-style Nop.Core, Nop.Data and
    Nop.Services (Requirement 2.4). The legacy
    <Project>{6BDA8332-939F-45B7-A25E-7A797260AE59}</Project>/<Name>Nop.Core</Name>,
    <Project>{CCD7F2C9-6A2C-4CF0-8E89-076B8FC0F144}</Project>/<Name>Nop.Data</Name> and
    <Project>{210541ad-f659-47da-8763-16f36c5cd2f4}</Project>/<Name>Nop.Services</Name>
